the 208 billion dollar-a-year hotel  industry is massively competitive  location branding and architecture are  all very important but in here is where  the competition gets particularly heated  hotel amenities pretty much everything  that's provided by hotels outside of the  room itself are an easy way for a hotel  to set themselves apart from the others  some amenities we come to take for  granted TV seems more like a minimal  requirement than an added feature at  this point some amenities seem obvious  but have never caught on  for some reason why are there three  different soaps and two kinds of lotions  but no toothpaste occasionally a chain  of hotels introduces an innovative  amenity and the other chains raced to  catch up and one-up each other creates  an intense period of amenity competition  let's look at some of the hotel  amenities wars throughout history and  see if maybe were in the midst of  another one right now the most recent  and intense amenity war centered around  bedding it started in 1999 when  Starwood's Weston introduced the  heavenly bed the floral print bed  spreads were replaced with white on  white bedding by Simmons with a  pillowtop mattress containing 900 coils  three sheets and a Down blanket  available in three different thicknesses  depending on your climate the move to  white on white bedding actually caused  the cleanliness scores for hotels to go  up not only did things look cleaner but  the hotels were actually forced to wash  the bedding more frequently since the  white bedding didn't hide dirt and  stains as well as the darker floral  patterns  pyat was not to be left behind they  fired back with the Grande bed a Sealy  Posturepedic mattress with down blankets  at 250 thread count triple  and none of this was cheap mariette  spent 190 million to upgrade beds at its  family of hotels each king-size bed at  Marriott featured 300 thread count  cotton sheets 7 pillows a fluffy  mattress cover and a white duvet each  chain and turn introduced their own take  on luxurious bedding the battle  culminating with the Pan Pacific  Vancouver installing $10,000  british-made hypnose duchess mattresses  but the war wouldn't end there it would  move to the bathroom before settling  down for a while  Weston spending over ten million dollars  to upgrade its bathroom amenities  when an amenity shows up in an amenity  war it doesn't always get accepted and  stick around in the 60s the battles were  fought over color televisions remote  controls and Clark radios and those are  here to stay but the sewing kits and  shoe horns of the 70s are relics of the  era even the minibar rising to  prominence in the 90s is being phased  out for the most part hotels choose  amenities they think will provide a  feeling of luxury at a low cost to the  hotel so they don't necessarily go for  the stuff you expect to have at home  sometimes in the race to set themselves  apart hotels introduce amenities that  border on the absurd wax for ceiling  letters cowboy hats and bandanas cats  that can visit your room video cameras  dictionaries in room exercise bikes and  rare books have all made an appearance  as amenities over the years and while  these things might seem like nice free  bonuses for guests it's important to  remember that you're paying for the cost  of the amenities somewhere the $15 worth  of soaps and coffee might be subsidized  by the $30 a night parking fee and  amenities can generate a massive amount  of waste hotels and travelers combined  throw out as many as 5 million used bars  of soap a day charity organizations like  clean the world are working to recycle  as much of this as possible  providing soap to places that need it  but currently only 20% of US hotels are  involved meaning the large majority of  soap is still being thrown away so when  can we expect the next amenity war we  might be on the brink of one at the  moment this year  localized skirmishes have erupted in  Nashville Tennessee leading to chef  prepared meals for pets your choice of a  buckwheat goose down or latex pillow and  recording studios all being offered as  amenities at various hotels in the area  and maybe the most interesting amenity  I've ever  come across humans in 2016  Weston started it's running concierge  program in an effort to bring local  runners around the hotel to provide a  more local atmosphere for the guests who  like to run ultimately hotel amenities  are a great case study for how  competition in a market can generate  both useful progress that benefits the  customer and ridiculous features that  will tell tale of around campfires for  generations thanks to Squarespace for  sponsoring this video  Squarespace is hands-down the best and  easiest way to build a website for your  hotel cat movie or YouTube channel I've  used Squarespace for a long time and I  just added a recommendations page to my  website  Thomas flight comm using Squarespace it  was easy to add books movies music and  YouTube videos to page in a way that  looks good no plugins or updates are  required ever if you want to check out  that page and see some of the things I  think are cool I'll link to it in the  description and you can start your free  trial today at squarespace.com  Thomas flight and use code Thomas flight  to get 10% off your first purchase or  use the link in the description below at  squarespace.com / thomas flight get  started building your website today  thanks so much for watching I hope you  enjoyed this video special thank you as  always to my patreon supporters go to  patreon.com/scishow on a slate for more  information and I'll talk to you again  next time
 

